    "interpretation": "The pinned R580 source gives a conditional route for a foreign tracker into the PMM wait. Migration can merge mapping pushes from multiple accessible GPU IDs into the VA-block tracker; block cleanup can then pass that tracker into the PMM root tracker. The opt-in patch now unlocks the VA block around ordinary no-eviction PMM allocation, retains a successful chunk on the retry list, reacquires the lock, and forces the caller to restart and revalidate. The stock/default allocation path remains unchanged. Thus the specific foreign-tracker wait is statically shown to be unlocked in isolated mode, while the conditional source route still exists. The wait polls a channel's GPU tracking semaphore through its CPU mapping and refreshes the cached value by reading the mapped payload. In sleepable context, the spin helper schedules after about 10 us; the source comment says this is required for RM interrupts and UVM fault service. The 30-second warning is diagnostic only, not a wait deadline. This is not proof of a deadlock or of safe cross-provider completion: source shows tracker entries resolve through their channel to a GPU-owned semaphore pool, and isolated mode skips peer GPU semaphore mappings while rejecting cross-GPU dependencies. Teardown removes tracker references, synchronizes PMM, destroys channels and then semaphore pools. These source-order checks do not prove runtime completion, CPU visibility/coherence, or absence of races. Provider-affine memory duplication still rejects cross-provider operations, and mixed-provider UVM address-space/runtime behavior remains unvalidated.",
